# Explore the Hidden Mosaic of a ReLU Network

A shallow ReLU network is best understood as a geometric switchboard: affine preactivations choose a Boolean pattern, that pattern chooses a cell, and the cell chooses an exact affine output formula. Start with the interactive walk below.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The orange switching locations separate three feasible patterns; the fourth formal two-bit pattern is impossible. Run the complete numerical tour next.

{{demo:0}}

## From a point to its exact local equation

The pattern-evaluation procedure performs one dense matrix–vector product and strict sign tests.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the precise feasibility theorem</summary>For a pattern $p$, its activation cell is nonempty exactly when there exists an input satisfying $z_j(x)>0$ for every active bit and $z_j(x)\le 0$ for every inactive bit. Each cell is an intersection of affine half-spaces and is therefore convex.</details>

## Extracting the affine branch

Once the active set is known, sum $v_jw_j$ for the slope and $v_jb_j$ for the intercept correction.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal why the formula is exact</summary>On an active coordinate, $\operatorname{ReLU}(z_j)=z_j$; on an inactive coordinate, $\operatorname{ReLU}(z_j)=0$. Substitution term by term gives $F(x)=g_p\cdot x+d_p$ throughout the cell.</details>

## Interpolation without approximation

Because a cell is convex, the segment between two of its points stays inside it. The output therefore interpolates exactly.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the affine-combination proof</summary>Every preactivation obeys $z_j(tx+(1-t)y)=tz_j(x)+(1-t)z_j(y)$. Keeping a fixed set of active summands preserves this identity, as do weighted sums and the output bias. Thus $F(tx+(1-t)y)=tF(x)+(1-t)F(y)$ whenever all three points share one pattern.</details>

## Searching the feasible mosaic

For exact continuous data, feasibility is a linear-inequality problem. For finite exploratory data, witness enumeration gives an immediate lower bound on realized patterns.

{{algorithm:2}}

For broader background, see the [ReLU overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rectifier_(neural_networks)), [convex sets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convex_set), and [linear programming](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_programming). The crucial distinction is between $2^k$ patterns that can be written and the subset that can actually be realized by an input.

